00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:53.580
have to load all of your
data at all to preview it.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.539
So you can just look
at a tiny piece.

00:16:55.539 --> 00:16:56.830
So that's what we've done here.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:02.550
I should of actually ask
before, is this legible back?

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:03.050
OK, good.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.500
And so you see now
we have a nice,

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:11.057
when the taxi was picked
up, where it was picked up,

00:17:11.057 --> 00:17:12.640
somewhere in New
York in the Lat long.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:19.710
In Datalab we can run
queries against BigQuery.

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:22.619
Also in Spark, you
can register SQL,

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:24.510
you can register SQL tables.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.910
So I'm just running
a simple select count

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:29.857
star against both
BigQuery and Spark

00:17:29.857 --> 00:17:31.440
to make sure that I
have all the data.

00:17:34.187 --> 00:17:36.520
It takes a long time in Spark
because this data is still

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:37.910
sitting in GCS.

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:38.700
Spark is lazy.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.399
So until I say
select count start,

00:17:40.399 --> 00:17:41.940
it will actually
never load the data.

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:49.530
Once that finishes,
what I want to do

00:17:49.530 --> 00:18:00.900
is I need to bucket my data into
the hour and day of the week,

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:04.500
so I can ask the question,
on a typical Thursday

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:08.070
morning at 10:00 AM, how
many taxicabs were there

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.500
in New York City?

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:13.320
And so this SQL query
just buckets my data

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:20.250
by hour, day of week, and
city block, essentially,

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:25.610
one thousandth of a GPS
coordinate, or of a degree

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:28.740
of latitude or longitude.

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:30.750
So you can see we
haven't lost any data.

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:33.670
We have all the records.

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:36.420
And we can start
machine learning.

00:18:39.081 --> 00:18:39.830
That needs to run.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:48.450
And, oh, that's all right.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:51.150
While that's running, I'd like
to introduce the Spark MLlib

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:52.630
API.

00:18:52.630 --> 00:18:57.120
MLlib's API, actually my
favorite thing about it,

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.490
beyond its convenience
and its use,

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.550
because I think
it's really slick.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:05.490
There are four main classes
in MLlib, transformers, which

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:09.537
take data and output new data.

00:19:09.537 --> 00:19:11.370
Really what they do is
they all add a column

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:12.161
to your data frame.

00:19:12.161 --> 00:19:15.210
So they just add a column
to your SQL data structure.

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:17.580
There are estimators
which take your data frame

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:18.770
and output a trained model.

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:25.360
So these are all the
algorithms I mentioned,

00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:29.550
the regressors, the
classifiers, the clusters.

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:33.840
Then there's the model
itself, which takes your data

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:36.290
and makes predictions
based on it.

00:19:36.290 --> 00:19:40.340
And the really neat thing
is predictions are just

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:41.690
another column of data.

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:43.940
And I said transformers are
things that take your data

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:44.930
and add a column.

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:47.900
So models are
transformers, which

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.810
means you can
compose them in just

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:53.000
a sequence of transformers,
and then an estimator,

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.430
you fit it to get a model.

00:19:54.430 --> 00:19:56.930
And you keep transforming.

00:19:56.930 --> 00:19:59.030
The way that you compose
them, the best way,

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:01.200
is inside a pipeline.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:03.462
And here is a picture
from, this is just

00:20:03.462 --> 00:20:04.670
from the Spark documentation.

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:09.070
You can find it, just
Spark ML pipelines.

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:12.870
But setting taxis
aside for a second,

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:15.530
suppose you had some
raw text, like say,

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.330
the works of Shakespeare.

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:20.240
And you wanted to classify
them into, say, categories,

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:23.150
comedies, tragedies, etc.

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:26.870
One way you could go about
this is you take your raw text,

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:30.170
you'd split it into
words, or possibly n-grams

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.360
depending on your model.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:36.980
Then you need to somehow
convert these words

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:40.810
into some kind of
numeric feature vector.

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:43.480
And then finally you train
an estimator on that.

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:46.320
So in this case, Spark
provides just plug and play,

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:49.390
a tokenizer, many different
things, but a hashing

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:52.900
transform, and a logistic
regression estimator.

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:55.180
You put all of these
together in a pipeline.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:56.582
And then you fit the pipeline.

00:20:56.582 --> 00:20:58.540
And you end up with your
trained model that you

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:00.830
can transform into predictions.

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:06.180
So this is again straight out of
the Spark's API documentation.

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:09.390
The high level is
we create toy code,

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:12.190
but it has this text column.

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:15.850
We define these
transforms and estimators,

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:20.040
all in like one line of
code and throw them together

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:23.600
into a pipeline,
fit it to our data.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:25.300
We get a model.

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:30.160
You run the model, or
you create toy test data.

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:33.340
And then you transform the
model to get predictions.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:35.680
So really this is,
in this case, it's

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:37.480
a very glorified word count.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:39.740
The data is designed
so that Spark

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:42.400
is, if the data contains
the word Spark, it's 1,

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:43.750
otherwise it's 0.

00:21:43.750 --> 00:21:47.380
But in general, you could do
more fancy transformations,

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:54.480
like topic determination
in the Shakespeare example.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:58.250
So if I scroll back
up, the last thing I

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:00.500
said when I
preprocessed data was

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:02.060
that I'd like to describe it.

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:04.910
I know this top
column is quite faint.

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:07.610
But here's the final
state of our data.

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:18.170
There's the hour
in which, the hour

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:21.350
in the day, between 0 and
23, when she was picked up,

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:23.030
the day of the week,
1 being Monday,

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:28.760
7 being Sunday, and our latitude
and longitude bucketized,

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:34.130
so rounded to the thousandth,
the count of the number

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:37.160
of pickups in this region.

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:41.720
And then just because it's
kind of unreasonable to train

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:45.510
a model from one to 5,000,
I took the log of that

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:48.170
so it's between 0 and 11,
which is more manageable.

00:22:52.570 --> 00:22:56.590
So now let's actually do
some machine learning.

00:22:56.590 --> 00:23:00.730
So this should look very similar
to the text processing example,

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:04.540
except we don't need to do
much transformation because I

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:06.640
chose to use a random
forest regressor.

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:08.290
And one of the wonderful
things about random forest

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:10.390
is they don't require
much pre-processing.

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:12.250
They can take numeric data.

00:23:12.250 --> 00:23:16.420
They don't need
normalize, or categorical,

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:20.180
or fancy transformations, which
Spark and MLlib can all do.

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:26.050
But so, all I do is take the
columns that I'm interested in,

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:28.570
I put them in a vector
called features.

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:32.500
I tell the regressor,
look at the features

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:35.890
and try to predict the
label column, which

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:41.720
is that log space feature
column, and run predictions.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:57.890
And if I run that now
we get some predictions.

00:23:57.890 --> 00:24:04.930
So some of these
are pretty good.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:06.180
There's only one pick up here.

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:08.070
It predicted a very low amount.

00:24:08.070 --> 00:24:11.350
This is a pretty
perfect, one for one.

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:17.800
But some of them are pretty
not so great, like this one.

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:19.570
So let's be more specific.

00:24:19.570 --> 00:24:24.980
I didn't mention evaluators
in my ML classes,

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:26.900
but they're also very important.

00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:29.470
So you can take the
evaluator and find

00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:31.650
that our R squared
values about one half,

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:38.410
and our root-mean-squared
error is about 1 and 1/2.

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:46.240
So in layman's terms, I mean
the root-mean-squared error

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:52.150
means that in logarithmic space
we're off by usually about 50%.

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:55.140
So if there were
six passengers, we

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:58.170
might think there is between
4 and 9, which isn't too bad.

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:04.189
Or sorry, not six passengers,
six taxis leaving in that hour.

00:25:04.189 --> 00:25:06.480
But I still don't have a
great sense of how good or bad

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:07.860
our model is.

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:11.910
So what I'm going to do
is rely on Cloud Datalab's

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:16.590
visualization
capabilities and sample

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:19.614
both the real data
and simulated data,

00:25:19.614 --> 00:25:21.780
where the simulated data
takes the number of pickups

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:24.150
that we predicted and
randomly generates them

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:25.900
inside that bucket.

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:29.420
So this is the
Google Maps library

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:31.390
that I loaded at the beginning.

00:25:31.390 --> 00:25:34.240
And it forms a very nice
Google Maps heat map.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:40.400
So this is what we predict
during rush hour at Thursday.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:44.080
We seem to have found
Manhattan, which is good.

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:48.160
But we have a lot of pickups
in the middle of East River.

00:25:48.160 --> 00:25:51.400
And it's generally
kind of messy.

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:55.680
If you compare this
to the real data,

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:58.540
you'll see this is a very nice
and pretty, because that's

00:25:58.540 --> 00:25:59.920
what the real data is.

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:01.660
It's all in the streets.

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:04.420
LaGuardia is all nice.

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:06.427
So clearly we have
some work to do.

00:26:06.427 --> 00:26:08.260
We can also look at the
middle of the night,

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:09.910
again on a Thursday.

00:26:09.910 --> 00:26:16.360
And it really is no better,
whereas reality is, again,

00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:18.610
quite nice and clean.

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:21.280
Also in real life, less
people are catching taxis

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:23.080
in the middle of the
night on a Thursday,

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:24.889
but we didn't really learn that.

00:26:24.889 --> 00:26:26.180
So how do we improve our model?

00:26:28.810 --> 00:26:35.550
So every estimator, but
also pretty much every class

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:41.400
in MLlib, has this
extractParamMap method,

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:44.670
where you can look at the
parameters inside of it.

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:47.030
So this is what our random
forest regressor ran

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:48.860
with in the default parameters.

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:53.360
I'll draw your attention to,
the trees are only of depth 5.

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:57.940
Trees are only of depth 5,
and there were 20 of them.

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.920
Not much of a forest, and
not much in terms of trees.

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:05.910
So we could go
and rerun the code

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:09.980
with a deeper tree,
or more trees.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:12.320
But then we have to
worry about overtraining

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:13.700
and all of these things.

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:18.580
So really the trick is to
let Spark do the work for you

00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:22.190
and set up a parameter
grid, and say,

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:28.480
please consider trees
of depth 5 and 15,

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:30.940
and set the number of
trees to both 5 and 20.

00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:34.450
Build this whole grid
and then evaluate.

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:39.160
There are a couple different
ways to run all of your grid.

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:42.040
I used a trainValidationSplit.

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:43.810
You could also use
cross-validation.

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:45.340
It's not a big deal.

00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:47.860
This is one case
where Spark does

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:49.870
take a long time, which
is why I kicked off all

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:53.720
my data processing beforehand.

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:57.080
Cross-validation takes a long
time because as a general rule,

00:27:57.080 --> 00:28:00.830
Spark will never transform
the same data twice

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:02.550
at the same time.

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:10.780
And so unfortunately when
you run your parameter grid,

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:14.300
it will only ever use one
piece of the grid at a time.

00:28:14.300 --> 00:28:18.610
And if you've scaled out as
I have, it's pretty fast.

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:19.880
But it's still not great.

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:28.190
So with this, I
took the best model

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:31.760
and evaluated it on
my validation data.

00:28:31.760 --> 00:28:35.810
And we got a better R
squared, 0.73, not the best

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:43.120
but our root-mean-squared error
is now getting more reasonable.

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:47.590
And if we go back and
look at our models,

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:52.670
we can re-simulate the data.

00:28:55.310 --> 00:28:58.311
And now this kind of
looks respectable.

00:28:58.311 --> 00:29:00.560
We don't have pickups in the
middle of the East River.

00:29:03.849 --> 00:29:05.390
Usually we have
pickups at LaGuardia.

00:29:05.390 --> 00:29:08.060
OK, that was bad, should
have fixed the seed.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:13.040
Anyways, but we found
Manhattan much better.

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:18.230
And it looks much more like the
real data, same with midnight.

00:29:22.324 --> 00:29:24.740
We still think that there are
more pickups than there are,

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:27.830
but at least they're at
reasonable locations.

00:29:27.830 --> 00:29:32.180
And for the sake of time, I
didn't set a huge parameter

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:33.960
grid or super deep trees.

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:38.810
But if you train on all the data
with super deep trees, or trees

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:41.720
of depth 30, which is
as deep as Spark goes,

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:50.537
you can get up above 90%, or an
R squared value of over 0.95.

00:29:50.537 --> 00:29:53.120
I kind of glossed over splitting
the data into test validation

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:55.370
and training, but I did.

00:29:55.370 --> 00:30:01.380
And here's the
actual test results.

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:04.129
And they're exactly in line with
our validation, which is good.

00:30:04.129 --> 00:30:05.295
We didn't overfit our model.

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:12.150
And pretty much
everything I've done here

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:15.770
could be done on
any Spark cluster

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:19.790
on GCE, assuming you had one.

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:23.960
But the last great
thing about MLlib

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:27.360
is the ability to take
models and reuse them later.

00:30:27.360 --> 00:30:32.510
So if you've seen talks on
ML Engine and version models,

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:35.300
and certain models in the
Cloud, this isn't exactly that,

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:38.480
but it's somewhat
similar in that we

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:42.250
can take the model
that we just trained,

00:30:42.250 --> 00:30:44.750
I don't even know what this
model is, it's the best

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:49.910
model that that grid put
out, and write it out to GCS,

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:55.880
and take any data, but in
this case, our test data,

00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:59.240
write that also out
to Cloud Storage.

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:03.440
And then after tearing down
our cluster in another cluster

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:07.670
a year from now, we can just
load the model, load our data,

00:31:07.670 --> 00:31:15.110
run our predictions, write
them out, write them out again,

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:18.510
evaluate them, do
whatever we like.

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:23.160
And at least these four are
completely agnostic as to what

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:25.290
we did in our data processing.

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:28.110
All I know is that I made
a Spark MLlib pipeline.

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:31.320
But these four lines of
code are absolutely reusable

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:34.160
over and over again.

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:40.110
And to demonstrate that,
if I go back to one of my,

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:46.060
go back to one of my
terminals, actually

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:50.160
if I go back to my
other Chrome browser,

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:57.680
I have this file in
GCS called predict.pi.

00:31:57.680 --> 00:32:00.670
And it just does
those four lines.

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:04.370
Load the model, loads
some data, transform

00:32:04.370 --> 00:32:06.940
the data using the model
to make predictions,

00:32:06.940 --> 00:32:08.630
save that out to
Cloud Storage again.

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:32.015
I can submit this as a job to
Cloud Dataproc using the jobs

00:32:32.015 --> 00:32:32.515
API.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.670
And the jobs API,
if your view Spark,

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:54.010
this is essentially the
same as running Spark a bit

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:56.776
on a Python application.

00:32:56.776 --> 00:32:58.900
There's too much log spam
that I should filter out.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:15.850
Actually, I was running
that on the same cluster.

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:18.100
I meant to run that
on a different cluster

00:33:18.100 --> 00:33:25.450
to demonstrate that I don't
need to know about anything

00:33:25.450 --> 00:33:27.239
that went on in that cluster.

00:33:27.239 --> 00:33:28.780
This is an entirely
separate cluster.

00:33:28.780 --> 00:33:31.990
You can't really
see that, but it is.

00:33:31.990 --> 00:33:35.950
And there we are.

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:39.570
And now it will take
that data, transform it,

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:40.990
write those predictions out.

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:43.510
And you can use those
predictions somewhere else.

00:33:43.510 --> 00:33:51.710
So if you were using Cloud
Datalab or any notebook,

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:53.510
generate a model in
an interactive, kind

00:33:53.510 --> 00:33:56.930
of ad hoc exploratory way,
you can save that model,

00:33:56.930 --> 00:34:01.910
send it off to another engineer,
another team, doesn't matter,

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:05.550
use that model over
and over again.

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:11.409
And that is really
a lot of log spam.

00:34:15.139 --> 00:34:16.900
But it says right
there, finished

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:24.530
with predictions, which was
the all done, successful job.

00:34:27.050 --> 00:34:28.569
And that's all I
have in the demo.

00:34:28.569 --> 00:34:30.110
So if we could go
back to the slides.

00:34:43.190 --> 00:34:56.650
To wrap things up,
hopefully you just

00:34:56.650 --> 00:35:00.070
saw how Spark and
Dataproc can leverage

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:06.500
Spark SQL to do very useful,
kind of ad hoc data processing,

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:13.520
MLlib to take that data
processing and add very simple,

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:21.260
three line machine learning
APIs on top of that.

00:35:21.260 --> 00:35:22.850
When you use it
with Datalab, you

00:35:22.850 --> 00:35:29.060
get that interactive,
just run, go back and edit

00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:30.920
your parameters options.

00:35:30.920 --> 00:35:36.530
Or you can use parameter
grid so you don't have to.

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:40.310
And that beautiful visualization
of Google Maps, Google Charts,

00:35:40.310 --> 00:35:42.660
and other things.

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:47.290
And lastly with the
Dataproc jobs API,

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:49.540
you can take all of
your trained models

00:35:49.540 --> 00:35:52.284
and reuse them elsewhere.

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:05.270
So I promised I'd talk
about other machine learning

00:36:05.270 --> 00:36:07.610
offerings, compare
and contrast them.

00:36:07.610 --> 00:36:09.596
So what about Cloud ML Engine?

00:36:12.790 --> 00:36:15.340
It's similar to MLlib
in many respects.

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:17.880
You train your models in
a distributed fashion.

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:20.650
You can persist the
models for later use.

00:36:20.650 --> 00:36:24.220
And the high level
API of TensorFlow,

00:36:24.220 --> 00:36:27.220
if you to the talks, one
of the high level APIs,

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:28.600
it's called tf.learn.

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:33.080
Both tf.learn and MLlib are
both based on scikit-learn.

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:35.480
So a lot of the
estimator model, that

00:36:35.480 --> 00:36:38.114
should also feel pretty
similar between the two.

00:36:38.114 --> 00:36:39.530
There are some
differences, but it

00:36:39.530 --> 00:36:42.590
should be pretty much the same.

00:36:42.590 --> 00:36:46.550
However, they do each
have key strengths.

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:48.400
ML Engine was written
for TensorFlow,

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:49.780
or it is TensorFlow.

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:53.860
So TensorFlow was written first
and foremost for deep learning.

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:57.130
So if you want deep
learning, I highly

00:36:57.130 --> 00:36:59.310
recommend TensorFlow
on ML Engine.

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:01.960
Spark does have deep learning.

00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:03.700
They do over neural nets.

00:37:03.700 --> 00:37:08.850
But it's been, or MLlib
does, but it's not

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:12.710
its intended
first-class purpose.

00:37:12.710 --> 00:37:16.490
ML Engine is server-less, sure
you've heard that word a lot

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:17.980
recently.

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:19.570
Dataproc is not.

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:22.970
Dataproc, you don't have
to maintain a server,

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:26.682
you can spin one up, take one
down, but you do have GC EVMs.

00:37:26.682 --> 00:37:29.060
And there's advantages
to both ways.

00:37:29.060 --> 00:37:32.080
One of the advantage
of being server-less is

00:37:32.080 --> 00:37:35.590
a user with a cell phone,
or a client on a cell phone,

00:37:35.590 --> 00:37:38.440
can make a call to
the Prediction API.

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:41.392
You don't have to worry about
managing infrastructure at all.

00:37:41.392 --> 00:37:42.850
The advantage of
having the cluster

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:47.380
around is you can have your
data cache in memory in Spark.

00:37:47.380 --> 00:37:50.320
And you can do
that kind of ad hoc

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:57.850
iterative exploratory analysis.

00:37:57.850 --> 00:38:01.210
Furthermore you can do
the kind of general Spark

00:38:01.210 --> 00:38:03.730
processing, or
pre-processing, on your data.

00:38:03.730 --> 00:38:07.780
So all that kind of painful SQL
munging I did at the beginning,

00:38:07.780 --> 00:38:10.600
you could do in Dataflow,
or Spark, and Beam,

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:14.230
and then run on ML Engine, but
it's all sitting there for you

00:38:14.230 --> 00:38:15.880
with Spark on Dataproc.

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:20.452
And the last key
advantage, or, I

00:38:20.452 --> 00:38:22.910
didn't mention the interactive
speed with the cache memory.

00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:30.090
The last key advantage of
MLlib is that all of the APIs

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:31.829
have 10 parameters.

00:38:31.829 --> 00:38:33.120
You don't need to specify them.

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:34.290
They work out of the box.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:35.880
You can auto tune them.

00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:39.790
And they can be written in
like the four lines of code

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:41.040
that I showed at the very end.

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:47.410
The last similarity
that I didn't

00:38:47.410 --> 00:38:50.920
mention when I was
mentioning the similarities,

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:53.530
is that both are
built on open source.

00:38:53.530 --> 00:38:56.020
So the keynote today,
there was a lot

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:59.110
of talk about how TensorFlow
is, in many respects, one

00:38:59.110 --> 00:39:02.140
of the hottest,
open source projects

00:39:02.140 --> 00:39:03.970
out there right now,
certainly if you

00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:06.700
look at GitHub statistics.

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:09.610
When I checked,
Spark and TensorFlow

00:39:09.610 --> 00:39:12.220
are the two most
forked GitHub projects.

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:14.140
So they're both very
similar in that respect.

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:20.640
And they're both still getting
incredible amount of commits,

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:22.260
TensorFlow just went 1.0.

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:24.910
Spark went 2.0 last
summer, but is continuing

00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:28.210
to iterate and evolve.

00:39:28.210 --> 00:39:30.100
So it's kind of really
an exciting time

00:39:30.100 --> 00:39:32.080
to be using either tool.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:34.240
And with [INAUDIBLE]
and Dataproc,

00:39:34.240 --> 00:39:40.570
you constantly leverage all of
the improvements that are being

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:42.291
driven in both communities.

00:39:45.730 --> 00:39:48.530
So that's essentially
all I have.

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:51.660
If I've encourage you to
go try any of this out,

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:54.760
I have some helpful links.

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:58.090
My demo is not available on any
of these links, but it will be.

00:39:58.090 --> 00:40:01.990
At some point these slides
will be available, I believe.

00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:08.262
And the demo should be linkable
through one of the quick starts

00:40:08.262 --> 00:40:09.970
there at the Cloud
Dataproc Quick Starts.

00:40:09.970 --> 00:40:12.287
We also have tutorials,
the initialization actions,

00:40:12.287 --> 00:40:13.745
including the
Datalab one I demoed.

00:40:16.600 --> 00:40:20.760
There's resources on Spark
ML and Cloud Datalab.

